From a493a6ad0738833adc5d5559871c49905c5d12ec Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hemant Kumar <heman10x@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 21:37:52 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] local: stream MoE experts off disk with real queue depth

Local research fork only. Not upstream, no PR intended.

When a MoE model is far larger than RAM, llama.cpp reaches routed experts through
the mmap of the GGUF shard, so every byte arrives as a 4 KB page fault. A page
fault is by construction blocking and one-at-a-time, and measured on this machine
the mmap path behaves exactly like a queue-depth-1 reader: 469 MB/s at QD 1
versus 717-801 MB/s at QD 2+, on identical bytes.

expert-stream.c issues all of a layer's expert reads at once with
FILE_FLAG_NO_BUFFERING | FILE_FLAG_OVERLAPPED and hands ggml pointers into its
own arena. The hook goes in ggml_compute_forward_mul_mat_id because
matrix_row_counts there is already the exact list of experts the call will use,
and it is complete before any expert byte is touched - the information needed to
fill the disk queue is simply sitting one barrier earlier than the reads it could
be driving. Tensors are matched by name against the manifest, so nothing is
plumbed through the loader.

Measured against the same prompt, cap and binary: 126.6 s -> 115.9 s wall, prompt
0.2 -> 0.3 t/s, 11,286 reads totalling 24.07 GiB, ZERO failed reads and ZERO
arena-full fallbacks, and greedy output identical to the mmap path. The zero
failures are the point of this stage: the manifest offsets, the sector-slop
arithmetic and the 32-byte alignment are all correct.

A DEADLOCK IS FIXED HERE AND THE SHAPE OF IT MATTERS. The first version
initialised lazily, setting g_init = -1 pessimistically on entry. This function
runs on every thread in the pool, and the call site uses the result to decide
whether to execute an extra ggml_barrier(). Threads calling during the init
window saw -1, answered "not an expert", and skipped the barrier while the
initialising thread took it - so one thread waited at a barrier its peers would
never reach. It presented as slow I/O: a 6 GB working set and 1.65 GB of reads,
which is what streaming is supposed to look like. What exposed it was CPU time,
+0.0 s over 12 s, when seven threads on a spin barrier should have burned ~84 s.
InitOnceExecuteOnce now blocks every caller until init completes so all threads
observe the same state and take the same branch. Control flow around a barrier
must be identical on every thread.

Deliberately does NOT cache: every expert is re-read on every use, so the
queue-depth effect is measured with one cause. Caching is the next stage and is
worth far more, but it means gigabytes of dirty private memory on a machine where
the model is 5x RAM, which deserves its own change.

Every failure path returns NULL and the caller falls back to the ordinary mmap
pointer, so a mis-parsed manifest, a failed read, a short read or a full arena
costs speed and never correctness. Off entirely unless V4F_EXPERT_MANIFEST is set.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
